            <ArticleTitle>The diagnostic value of soluble CD163 in patients presenting with chest pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1662-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinbiochem.2009.06.028</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">CD163 is a scavenger receptor for the uptake of haptoglobin-hemoglobin (Hpt-Hb) complexes. The Hpt-Hb complexes are being formed in the plaque in response to intraplaque hemorrhage, a hallmark of atherosclerotic plaque instability. We therefore investigated whether soluble CD163 (sCD163) was elevated in patients with an acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">All subjects presenting with chest pain suggestive of myocardial ischemia referred to either the emergency department or the coronary care unit were included in a prospective follow-up study. Plasma was collected and frozen at -80 degrees C until assayed. sCD163 was measured using a commercially available Elisa assay.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of 526 included chest pain patients, the final diagnosis was non-cardiac chest pain in 244 (46%) patients, non-STEMI in 67 (13%), and STEMI in 215 (41%). The non-STEMI patients were older, used more medication, had undergone more often coronary interventions, but did not differ with respect to risk factors, except for a higher incidence in dyslipidemia. Unexpectedly, sCD163 did not differentiate between patients with non-STEMI or STEMI and the non-cardiac chest pain patients (2.09+/-0.76 versus 2.24+/-0.86).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although ACS is characterized by intraplaque hemorrhage, the amount of intraplaque Hb release seems not to be substantial enough to result in a measurable difference in sCD163.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
